.TH CMDSPECS 1
.SH NAME
cmdspecs \- blackbox testing of Unix command line tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmdspecs
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR TEST ]...\&
.RB [ \-T ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR WHEN ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.IR DIR ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmdspecs
blackbox tests Unix command line tools.
Given some test scripts, their inputs, and expected outputs,
it verifies that the command line produces the expected output.
If not, it reports problems, and shows the differences.
.PP
A
.I test case
is a script plus the files that describe what it should do and
produce.
Test cases live in a
.I test directory
and
.B cmdspecs
is given one or more test directories as the
.I DIR
arguments.
At least one is required.
.SS Test files
Each test case has a name, here
.IR foo ,
and consists of the following files, all in the test directory:
.TP
.I foo.script
A script to run the test.
This is required.
.TP
.I foo.stdin
The file fed to standard input.
If it is missing, standard input is empty.
.TP
.I foo.stdout
The expected standard output.
If it is missing, the output is expected to be empty.
.TP
.I foo.stderr
The expected standard error.
If it is missing, the error output is expected to be empty.
.TP
.I foo.exit
The expected exit status.
If it is missing, the exit status is expected to be zero.
A script killed by signal
.I N
has the exit status 128+\fIN\fR, as in a shell.
.TP
.I foo.setup
A script to run before the test.
.TP
.I foo.teardown
A script to run after the test.
.PP
Usually, a single test is not enough.
All the tests are put into the same directory,
and they may share some setup and teardown code:
.TP
.I setup_once
A script to run once, before any tests.
.TP
.I setup
A script to run before each test.
.TP
.I teardown
A script to run after each test.
.TP
.I teardown_once
A script to run once, after all tests.
.PP
Apart from
.IR foo.script ,
all of these files are optional.
If a setup or teardown script is missing, it is simply not executed.
.PP
The scripts are usually shell scripts, but any executable program
will do.
.B cmdspecs
executes them directly, so they must have the execute permission set,
and a script needs a
.B #!\&
line to choose its interpreter.
They are run in the current directory of
.BR cmdspecs ,
not in the test directory,
and a script can find that directory in the
.B SRCDIR
environment variable.
The setup and teardown scripts always get an empty standard input.
.SS Execution order
Each test directory has its own setup and teardown scripts,
which are run only for the tests in that directory.
For each directory,
.B cmdspecs
does the following:
.IP 1. 3
Execute
.IR setup_once .
.IP 2. 3
For each test case
.IR foo ,
in turn:
.RS 3
.IP a. 3
Execute
.IR setup .
.IP b. 3
Execute
.IR foo.setup .
.IP c. 3
Execute the command by running
.IR foo.script ,
with standard input coming from
.IR foo.stdin ,
and capture its standard output, standard error and exit status.
.IP d. 3
Execute
.IR foo.teardown .
.IP e. 3
Execute
.IR teardown .
.IP f. 3
Report the result of the test:
does the exit status match
.IR foo.exit ,
the standard output match
.IR foo.stdout ,
and the standard error match
.IR foo.stderr ?
.RE
.IP 3. 3
Execute
.IR teardown_once .
.SS Failure handling
A test fails if its output does not match, and also when one of its
setup or teardown scripts fails.
The output of a failed setup or teardown script is shown with the
failure; see
.BR \-s .
In detail:
.TP
.I setup_once
If it fails, no more tests are run for that directory and
.I teardown_once
is not run.
.TP
.I setup
If it fails, the test fails and none of
.IR foo.setup ,
.IR foo.script ,
.I foo.teardown
and
.I teardown
are run.
.TP
.I foo.setup
If it fails, the test fails.
.I foo.script
and
.I foo.teardown
are not run, but
.I teardown
is.
.TP
.I foo.script
If it fails or its results do not match the expected ones,
the test fails, and both
.I foo.teardown
and
.I teardown
are still run.
.TP
.IR foo.teardown ", " teardown
If either fails, the test fails.
.TP
.I teardown_once
If it fails, the run fails, but the test results are not affected.
.PP
A test also fails if
.I foo.script
is missing or
.I foo.exit
is not valid.
Then
.I foo.setup
and
.I foo.teardown
are not run, but
.I setup
and
.I teardown
are.
.SS Windows
On Windows,
.B cmdspecs
needs the
.B sh
of Git for Windows or MSYS2 in
.BR PATH .
Windows ignores the
.B #!\&
line, so
.B cmdspecs
runs each script with that
.BR sh ,
which handles the line like Unix does.
There a script needs no execute permission,
and one without a
.B #!\&
line is run by
.BR sh .
.PP
Run
.B cmdspecs
from Git Bash, or from cmd or PowerShell as
.BR "python path\eto\ecmdspecs" ,
as
.B pip install
makes no
.I cmdspecs.exe
for it.
.PP
The output is compared byte for byte.
Native Windows programs write CRLF line endings,
so the expected output files of their tests need them too,
while the tools of Git for Windows write LF.
Git for Windows checks out text files with CRLF by default,
so mark the test files with
.B \-text
in
.I .gitattributes
to have them checked out as they were committed.
.PP
.B DATADIR
and
.B SRCDIR
are Windows paths, which
.B sh
accepts, but
.BR pwd (1)
in a script shows a path in another form, such as
.IR /c/Users/name .
.PP
Windows can't remove a file which a process has open,
so when
.I foo.script
exits,
.B cmdspecs
kills what it left running,
as that could keep the output files of the test open.
What the setup and teardown scripts leave running is not killed.
On a timeout or an interrupt, a script and its child processes get a
Ctrl-Break instead of
.BR SIGTERM ,
and are killed if they are still running two seconds later.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test =\fITEST\fR
Run only the test named
.IR TEST ,
which is the
.I foo
of
.IR foo.script ,
without the directory.
The tests in all the given directories with that name are run.
Can be given many times.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-timings
Report how long the whole run and each test took,
and each script of a test,
after the test summary.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-timeout =\fISECONDS\fR
Kill a test, or a setup or teardown script, which runs longer than
.I SECONDS
(which can be fractional).
A test which timed out is reported as failed, and the output it wrote
before that is kept in the
.I \-actual
files.
A script which timed out fails like one that exited with an error.
The script and its child processes are sent
.B SIGTERM
and, if they are still running after two seconds,
.BR SIGKILL ;
see
.B Windows
above for how this works there.
By default there is no limit.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep
Keep the temporary directory on failure.
It is kept if a test fails, a
.B setup_once
or
.B teardown_once
script fails, or
.B cmdspecs
is interrupted or hits an internal error.
Its location is shown on a line starting with
.BR "Test work area:" .
The line is after the test summary,
or on stderr when there is no summary.
The value of
.B DATADIR
is the
.I data
directory in it.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-no\-capture
Do not capture the output of the setup and teardown scripts.
By default their output is captured and shown only when the script
fails, as part of the failure.
With this option it goes straight to the output of
.BR cmdspecs ,
which can disturb the progress display.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show the name and result of each test on a line of its own,
instead of a dot for each passed test and
.B F
for each failed one.
In this form the name is shown as
.IR DIR :: foo .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-color =\fIWHEN\fR
Color the output.
.I WHEN
is
.BR auto ,
.BR yes ,
or
.BR no .
The default,
.BR auto ,
colors the output only when it goes to a terminal and the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is unset or empty.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version number and exit.
For a git build, the version ends with a short hash of the script,
for example
.BR 0.33+git.3f9a1c2d ,
so that bug reports can name the exact build.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The exit statuses follow those of
.BR pytest (1)
where that makes sense.
.TP
.B 0
All tests passed.
.TP
.B 1
At least one test failed, a
.I setup_once
or
.I teardown_once
script failed, a file could not be read or written, for example
the output or the temporary directory, or on Windows no
.B sh
was found.
.TP
.B 2
.B cmdspecs
was interrupted.
.TP
.B 3
An internal error: a bug in
.BR cmdspecs .
A traceback is shown.
Please report it.
.TP
.B 4
The command line was invalid:
an unknown option, a directory which could not be read, or a
.B \-\-test
name which matches no test.
No tests are run.
.TP
.B 5
A directory on the command line contains no tests.
This is not checked with
.BR \-\-test ,
and the tests of the other directories are still run.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B cmdspecs
sets the following variables for all the scripts it runs:
.TP
.B DATADIR
A temporary directory where files may be created by the test.
There is only one such directory for the whole run:
all tests in all directories share it,
and it is not emptied between tests.
It is removed when
.B cmdspecs
exits, unless
.B \-\-keep
is used and the run fails or is interrupted.
.TP
.B TESTNAME
The name of the current test.
It is empty for
.B setup_once
and
.BR teardown_once .
.TP
.B SRCDIR
The directory from which
.B cmdspecs
was launched.
.PP
.B cmdspecs
itself reads
.BR NO_COLOR ,
which turns off colored output when set to a non-empty value
and
.B \-\-color
is
.BR auto .
.SH FILES
When an output does not match,
.B cmdspecs
writes files next to the test's own files,
in the test directory:
.TP
.IR foo.stdout\-actual ", " foo.stderr\-actual
The actual standard output and standard error of
.IR foo.script .
.TP
.IR foo.stdout\-diff ", " foo.stderr\-diff
The differences between the expected and the actual output.
.PP
These files are not removed.
Do not commit them to version control.
.SH EXAMPLES
To test that the
.BR echo (1)
command outputs the expected string,
create a directory and a file called
.I echo\-tests/hello.script
in it, containing the following content:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/sh
echo hello, world
.fi
.RE
.PP
The script must be executable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mkdir echo\-tests
$ chmod +x echo\-tests/hello.script
.fi
.RE
.PP
Also create the file
.I echo\-tests/hello.stdout
containing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hello, world
.fi
.RE
.PP
Then you can run the tests:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cmdspecs echo\-tests
================= cmdspecs session starts =================
platform linux \-\- Python 3.13.5, cmdspecs X.Y
srcdir: /home/user
datadir: /tmp/cmdspecs\-ab12cd34/data
keep: no
testdirs: echo\-tests
collected 1 test

echo\-tests .                                          [100%]
==================== 1 passed in 0.00s =====================
.fi
.RE
.PP
The header shows the values of
.B SRCDIR
and
.BR DATADIR ,
whether
.B \-\-keep
is enabled, the test directories and, with
.BR \-\-test ,
the selected tests.
.PP
If you change the stdout file to be something else,
.B cmdspecs
will report the differences:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cmdspecs echo\-tests
================= cmdspecs session starts =================
platform linux \-\- Python 3.13.5, cmdspecs X.Y
srcdir: /home/user
datadir: /tmp/cmdspecs\-ab12cd34/data
keep: no
testdirs: echo\-tests
collected 1 test

echo\-tests F                                          [100%]

========================= FAILURES =========================
__________________________ hello ___________________________
stdout diff:
\-\-\- echo\-tests/hello.stdout    2025\-01\-01 10:00:00.000000 +0000
+++ echo\-tests/hello.stdout\-actual    2025\-01\-01 10:00:01.000000 +0000
@@ \-1 +1 @@
\-something else
+hello, world
================= short test summary info ==================
FAILED hello \- stdout diff:
==================== 1 failed in 0.00s =====================
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.I echo\-tests
directory now also contains
.I hello.stdout\-actual
with the actual output, and
.I hello.stdout\-diff
with the differences.
If the actual output is correct, you can rename it to be the expected
file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mv echo\-tests/hello.stdout\-actual echo\-tests/hello.stdout
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is a convenient way of creating the expected output files:
write the script, run it, check by hand that the actual output is
correct, and then rename the file.
